      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, GridSearchCV\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix, classification_report, accuracy_score\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\n\n# Load digits dataset\ndigits = load_digits()\nX, y = digits.data, digits.target\n\nprint(f\"Dataset shape: {X.shape}\")  # 1797 samples, 64 features\nprint(f\"Classes: {np.unique(y)}\")\nprint(f\"Class distribution:\\n{pd.Series(y).value_counts().sort_index()}\")\n\n# Visualize sample digits\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 5, figsize=(12, 5))\nfor idx, ax in enumerate(axes.flat):\n    image = X[idx].reshape(8, 8)\n    ax.imshow(image, cmap='gray')\n    ax.set_title(f'Label: {y[idx]}')\n    ax.axis('off')\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n# Feature statistics\nprint(f\"\\nFeature range: [{X.min():.1f}, {X.max():.1f}]\")\nprint(f\"Feature mean: {X.mean():.2f}, std: {X.std():.2f}\")\n\n# Split data\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.25, random_state=42)\n\n# Scale features (critical for SVMs)\nscaler = StandardScaler()\nX_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)\nX_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)\n\nprint(f\"\\nTraining set: {X_train_scaled.shape}\")\nprint(f\"Test set: {X_test_scaled.shape}\")"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "# Train SVMs with different kernels on a subset (full dataset takes time)\nsubset_size = 400\nX_subset = X_train_scaled[:subset_size]\ny_subset = y_train[:subset_size]\n\nkernels = ['linear', 'rbf', 'poly', 'sigmoid']\nmodels = {}\naccuracies = {}\n\nfor kernel in kernels:\n    print(f\"Training {kernel} kernel SVM...\")\n    \n    svm = SVC(kernel=kernel, random_state=42)\n    svm.fit(X_subset, y_subset)\n    \n    train_acc = svm.score(X_subset, y_subset)\n    test_acc = svm.score(X_test_scaled, y_test)\n    \n    models[kernel] = svm\n    accuracies[kernel] = {'train': train_acc, 'test': test_acc}\n    \n    print(f\"  Training accuracy: {train_acc:.4f}\")\n    print(f\"  Test accuracy: {test_acc:.4f}\")\n\n# Compare kernels\nkernel_df = pd.DataFrame(accuracies).T\nprint(\"\\nKernel Comparison:\")\nprint(kernel_df)\n\n# RBF typically works best for non-linear problems\nbest_kernel = kernel_df['test'].idxmax()\nprint(f\"\\nBest performing kernel: {best_kernel}\")"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "# Important SVM hyperparameters:\n# C: regularization strength (lower = more regularization, underfitting)\n# gamma: RBF kernel parameter (higher = complex boundaries, overfitting)\n\nparam_grid = {\n    'C': [0.1, 1, 10, 100],\n    'gamma': ['scale', 'auto', 0.001, 0.01, 0.1],\n}\n\n# Use a subset for faster grid search\nsubset_train = X_train_scaled[:600]\nsubset_y_train = y_train[:600]\n\n## Grid Search for Hyperparameter Tuning\n\nImportant SVM hyperparameters: C (regularization strength, lower = more regularization, underfitting) and gamma (RBF kernel parameter, higher = complex boundaries, overfitting).\n\nGrid search systematically tests all parameter combinations with cross-validation. The best parameters balance fitting complexity (C) with generalization. SVMs store support vectors (points near the boundary); if this percentage is high (>50%), the model is complex and may overfit. Interpretation: support vectors are the \"decision boundary points\"; fewer means a simpler, more interpretable boundary.\n\nThe trade-off: C controls how much the model tolerates training errors. Low C forces the model to be smooth even if it misclassifies training points (high regularization, simple boundary). High C permits errors to minimize training loss (low regularization, complex boundary). Gamma controls RBF kernel width: low gamma = smooth, far-reaching influence; high gamma = sharp, local influence. Together they determine model complexity."
        ]
      ]
